which has lead to their applications in detergents and watersofteners. Advances in inorganic porous frameworks based on other elements have been reviewed recently.2a Such materials include metal phosphates, sulfides and chlorides. An early report also exists of the use of cyanide as a bridging group, to form the mixed-metal open framework $\mathrm{K_{2}Z n_{3}[F e(C N)_{6}]_{2}}.x\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , in which tetrahedral $\scriptstyle{Z\ n^{2+}}$ ions link together octahedral hexacyanoferrates.2b The analogy between this compound and zeolites was pointed out by the authors, who described the structure as having large cavities occupied by $\mathrm{K^{+}}$ counterions and water molecules. Since the early 1990s, research into materials with polymeric, sometimes porous, structures based on metal ions and organic bridging ligands has increased greatly.3 Early papers by Robson,4 Moore,5 Yaghi,6 and Zaworotko7 pointed out the rich possibilities for new material structures and properties offered by these coordination polymers. In principle, through the wide choice of metal, and the infinite choice and design of ligands, a broad range of structural, magnetic, electrical, optical, and catalytic properties might be rationally incorporated into such materials. The number of papers which feature the term ‘coordination polymer’ has increased dramatically over the last few years (see Figure 1). Use of the term ‘metal-organic framework’ is also increasing, although perhaps delayed by a few years. The first term is used widely to describe any extended structure based on metals and organic bridging ligands, whereas the second is normally, though not exclusively, applied to such structures which exhibit porosity. The establishment of porosity in these polymeric metal-organic structures has been a challenging but central goal during the development of this field since, by analogy with zeolites, it opens up possibilities for new chemical separations, ion exchange, sensing and possibly even catalytic behaviour. This review attempts to give an accessible summary of the field at a stage where trends can be identified in synthetic approaches, characterisation of material properties is becoming more detailed, and some properties have been observed which point toward possible longer term applications. It has not been possible to include every contribution to the topic. Instead I have tried to include illustrative examples of key aspects.
## 2 Synthesis
## 2.1 The first problem – predicting network geometry
In order to make a coordination polymer it is, in principle, only necessary to react a potentially bridging ligand with a metal ion which has more than one vacant or labile site (Figure 2). Either infinite extended polymeric or discrete closed oligomeric structures can arise, depending on the nature of the system used. In particular, if ‘divergent’ metal sites are coordinated (e.g. mutually trans positions, or all four sites of a tetrahedral ion) then an extended polymer results. The most detailed structural information will be gained from single crystal X-ray crystallography. Indeed, without knowing the crystal structure, it is going to be very hard to interpret any subsequently observed properties. Therefore, synthesis in this area is strongly directed toward obtaining $\mathbf{\boldsymbol{X}}$ -ray quality single crystals. Amorphous solid phases and gels have not been greatly investigated, despite the potential for novel chemistry here too. Avoidance of an amorphous product is favoured if the metal–ligand combination used is highly labile, i.e. bond formation is rapidly reversible, so that initially formed (kinetic) products, which may be mixtures, have the opportunity to rearrange and give a single thermodynamically favoured product. This is a sort of ‘self-assembly’ approach. Amongst the more labile metal ions are $\mathrm{Cu^{+}}$ , $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ , $\mathbf{A}\mathbf{g}^{+}$ , $\mathrm{Cd}^{2+}$ , $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ , $\scriptstyle\mathbf{Co}^{2+}$ and $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ and these feature prominently in the field of coordination polymers. A potential problem with labile metal ions is, however, that they often do not impose as strong a preference for a given geometry as do other ions. This can therefore lead to a lack of predictability over the structure of the network obtained. Another potential problem is flexibility in the bridging ligand. Clearly, if a ligand has a number of possible conformations, again, framework geometry will be hard to predict. In a study of ligand flexibility, using 1,2-bis(4- pyridyl)ethane (Figure 3), which can adopt gauche or anti conformations, it was shown that a variety of network geometries can indeed occur. Reaction of this ligand with $\mathrm{Co}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}$ under a range of conditions lead to the isolation of three different isomeric polymers, all of formula ${\lbrack{\bf C o}_{2}({\bf N}_{-}}$ $\mathrm{O}_{3})_{4}(\mathrm{L})_{3}]_{n}$ , which also had similar asymmetric units in their crystal structures,8a and the same trigonal bipyramidal $N_{3}O_{2}$ coordination sets at the metals. The three products differed in having ligand conformations that were either all anti, anti-antigauche, or gauche-gauche-anti, each of which propagated into contrasting network topologies, one of which is shown in Figure 3. It should also be noted that other structure-determining factors seem to be very closely balanced, so that the concentration, type of anion, temperature etc. are all variables which can affect the overall structure obtained. An additional problem is that a single metal coordination geometry can also propagate into more than one type of network.8b
## 2.2 Approaches to the first problem
Use of rigid ligands. Orientational freedom of the ligand lone pairs may be reduced by using rigid backbones, such as in $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -bipyridine (1) (Figure 4). Although this ligand can in principle rotate about the central C–C bond, importantly this rotation does not affect the mutual orientation of the two lone pairs. Most of the early work on coordination polymers (and much of that still done) employs rigid ligands such as this. Examples of rigid 3- and 4-connecting N-donor ligands which have also been used are given in Figure 4. A rigid potentially 4-connecting ligand is hexamethylenetetramine (3) as employed by Ciani et al.9 An early example of a rigid fourconnecting ligand is tetra(4-cyanophenyl)methane (4).4a Examples of various rigid carboxylates which have been used are also shown, and Lin has made use of the hybrid pyridine-carboxylate ligand isonicotinate, $\scriptstyle4-\mathrm{NC}_{5}\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{CO}_{2}-$ .10
Exploitation of recurring coordination motifs. Despite the fact that coordination at the metal is often quite flexible, it is sometimes possible to identify recurring structural motifs either from existing polymeric structures, or from the much greater number of discrete model complexes. A structural motif which is common for a variety of metals in discrete complexes and which has been found to recur in coordination polymers11 is the dimetallic tetracarboxylate ${\bf M}_{2}({\bf O}_{2}{\bf C R})_{4}$ unit (Figure 5, above). Several bridging di- and tri-carboxylates have been shown to react with $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ ions to give networks based on this unit.11 An example is that based on $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ and 1,3,5-benzenetricarboxylate (6) (the trianion of trimesic acid) (Figure 5, below).11d When a given structural unit which assembles from a number of metal ions and ligands can be identified, it is sometimes referred to as a secondary building unit, or SBU11a–c,12 (the ligands, metal ions and anions themselves are the primary building units). This is analogous to aluminosilicate zeolite chemistry, where nine SBUs based on tetrahedral $\mathrm{AlO}_{4}$ and $\mathrm{SiO}_{4}$ primary building units have been classified.1a The dicopper tetracarboxylate unit can be described as a square SBU11 with regard to the disposition of the R groups.
Tetrahedral $\mathrm{N}_{4}$ coordination at individual $\mathrm{{Cu(\mathrm{{I})}}}$ and $\mathrm{Ag(\mathrm{_I)}}$ centres with non-bulky N-donor bridging ligands has occurred in many cases, in particular in the large number of ‘diamondoid’ $\mathbf{ML}_{2}$ networks based on these metals with rod-like bidentate pyridine- and nitrile-derived ligands (Figure 6, above).13a–d However, despite being crystallised under similar conditions to diamondoid networks, the copper network based on 1,4-bis(4- pyridyl)butadiyne (10) proved to be the exception to the rule, giving instead a ladder-type structure with $\mathrm{{Cu(\mathrm{{I})}}}$ centres coordinated to three of the pyridine-type ligands and one acetonitrile ligand.
This product, of overall stoichiometry $\{\mathrm{Cu}_{2}(\mathrm{NCCH}_{3})(\mathbf{10})_{3}\}$ was even obtained if 10 was present in large excess.14a Also, the only crystallographically characterised example of trigonalplanar $\mathbf{Cu}(\mathrm{pyridine})_{3}$ -type coordination was observed in a polymeric complex $\mathrm{Cu(1)}_{1.5}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{1.5}.14i$ b With low coordination numbers it is also possible for metal–metal bonding to occur between $\mathrm{d}^{10}$ ions. An example is in the polymer $\mathrm{Ag}({\bf1})(\mathrm{NO}_{3}).$ .14c In this case, linear chains formed by $\mathrm{\bfN-Ag-N}$ coordination were cross-linked by $\operatorname{Ag}\cdots\operatorname{Ag}$ bonds of $2.970(\breve{2})\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ (Figure 6, below).
ROP relationships. Recently, where bridging bidentate phosphines were used, coordination polymers were obtained which had a clear structural relationship with their discrete solution-based precursors. In particular, triply-bridged complexes $[\mathrm{Ag}_{2}(\mathbf{11})_{3}][\mathrm{anion}]_{2}$ (Figure 7) were characterised in solution by NMR spectroscopy and molecular weight determination.15a They were also obtained as crystals and characterised by $X\mathrm{.}$ -ray crystallography. If these crystals were left for several weeks in their supernatant, they slowly transformed via dissolution and recrystallisation into a second product, found to be the polymer shown in Figure 7. It is interesting that the precursor complex and this polymer are formally related to each other by ring-opening polymerisation (ROP). Ring-opening polymerisation is well-known in main-group chemistry, but is a novel development for coordination rings and cages, the key difference being that dative covalent bonds rather than ordinary covalent bonds are involved. Simultaneously, a similar structural relationship was suggested for related gold complexes, although the solution-state precursor was not fully characterised.15b The ability to form discrete complexes as well as polymers is due to the rotational freedom of the lone pairs of these ligands. Given the highly labile nature of the metal ions used, it may seem unlikely that very long chain structures would be sufficiently stable in solution to result in crystallisation of the observed linear polymers. Perhaps the existence of a ROP relationship actually indicates that ROP occurs at the surface of the growing polymeric crystal. In this way, ring-opened cages would become trapped as relatively insoluble polymeric material. It will be interesting to see if the concept of ROP leads to increased structural predictability and control in coordination polymers, and whether it sheds more light on the actual coordination polymer crystallisation processes. Although few studies have involved examination of the solution state precursors to coordination polymers, in at least one case15c the precursors, although discrete coordination cages, which must have undergone ring-opening in the process of producing the final polymer, were not structurally related to the final polymeric product by simple ROP. Therefore if ROP was indeed occurring, there would need to be an additional cage intermediate to those observed by NMR spectroscopy.
Effect of the anion. Anions may play key roles in determining the polymer structure. Apart from obvious differences in volume and shape which must be accommodated in the crystal lattice, the different coordinating abilities of anions, and their ability to bridge between metal centres (i.e. to act as bridging ligands themselves) may be important. For example, the helical staircase structure of $[\mathrm{Ag}(\mathbf{1}\mathbf{2})(\mathrm{NO}_{3})]$ $(12~=~\mathrm{bis}(4-$ pyridyl)tetrazine) was ascribed to the ability of $\mathrm{NO}_{3}-$ to bridge between $\mathbf{A}\mathbf{g}^{+}$ centres, whereas fluoro anions $\mathrm{BF_{4}}^{-}$ and $\mathrm{PF}_{6}-$ coordinate much more weakly and so metal–anion bonding interactions in $\ensuremath{[\mathrm{Ag}(\mathbf{1}2)}$ (fluoroanion)] were not thought to be structure determining.16 The latter two structures have solvent coordinated to the metals in place of the anions and the linear chains pack in a simple parallel manner (Figure 8).
## 2.3 The second problem – interpenetration
Having established the possibility of forming a two- or threedimensional coordination network, with some prediction as to its connectivity, use of long bridging ligands could, it seems, lead to structures with very large open cavities, far larger than those in aluminosilicate zeolites. However, as pointed out by Robson,4b and later demonstrated in a series of diamondoid structures by a number of groups, $13a-d$ what typically happens instead is that a second polymer network interpenetrates the first. The situation is illustrated in Figure 9. Such mutual interpenetration $_{13e,f}$ will typically fill up the potential cavities, reducing or even removing porosity. In fact, increasing the length of the bridging ligand leads to larger and larger natural cavity sizes, but the result is that increasing numbers of networks then mutually interpenetrate. For example, although the cyanide-bridged network in $\mathrm{Cd}(\mathrm{CN})_{2}$ exhibits twofold interpenetration (Figure 9, above), $4a$ the use of 1 as a bridge gave fourfold interpenetrated structures $\mathrm{Cu}(\mathbf{1})_{2}\mathrm{PF}_{6}\mathrm{1}3a$ and $\mathrm{Ag}(\mathbf{1})_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\mathrm{SCF}_{3}$ .13b Increasing the ligand length further with a trans-alkenediyl spacer in 1,2-trans-bipyridylethene (13) gave a fivefold interpenetrated network in $\mathrm{Cu}(\mathbf{13})_{2}\mathrm{BF}_{4}$ ,13c (Figure 9, below) and ninefold interpenetration occurred in $\mathrm{Ag}(\mathbf{14})_{2}\mathrm{PF}_{6}$ $\mathbf{\langle14_{\lambda}=4,4^{\prime}}$ -biphenyldicarbonitrile).17
An alternative way for a structure to self-fill was pointed out by Robson and co-workers.18 The polymer $\mathrm{\:\left\{Cu_{2}(O_{2}C C H-\right.}}$ $\mathrm{\boldmath~\Gamma~}_{3})_{4}\}_{3}({\bf2})_{2}]{\cdot}2\mathrm{\bfMeOH}$ $\begin{array}{r l}{(2}&{{}=}\end{array}$ tri(4-pyridyl)triazine) consists of chickenwire sheets with very large $3.4\mathrm{-}3.7~\mathrm{~nm}$ hexagonal windows (Figure 10). Despite these features seeming easily large enough for interpenetration to occur, the structure was instead found to self-fill by distorting its layers from planarity. By adopting puckered conformations, the layers allowed their vertices to protrude into the windows of adjacent layers.
## 2.4 Approaches to the second problem
Robson et al. demonstrated a way of avoiding interpenetration in a key early paper in the field of coordination polymers.4b Neutral frameworks $\mathrm{Cd}(\mathrm{CN})_{2}$ and $Z\mathrm{n}(\mathrm{CN})_{2}$ were found to exist as doubly interpenetrated diamondoid nets. However, a charged framework would also have to accommodate counterions. The framework salt $\mathrm{[N(CH_{3})_{4}][C u Z n(C N)_{4}]}$ was therefore prepared, and, as hoped, the bulky ammonium counterions present within the adamantoid cavities, prevented interpenetration. Importantly, for charge balance, only half of the adamantane cavities in the mixed-metal framework were required to be filled by cations, and the remaining cavities were apparently empty (there was no evidence of guests from $\mathbf{\boldsymbol{X}}$ -ray crystallography). It can also be reasoned that if strong enough interactions between the framework and guest molecules exist, then framework cavities may preferentially fill with guests than with additional polymer strands. This was suggested to have occurred in $\mathrm{Ni}({\bf1})_{2.5}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{2}(\mathrm{ClO}_{4})_{2}{\cdot}1.5({\bf1}){\cdot}2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O},^{19}$ in which $\mathrm{\nabla{X}}\mathrm{\cdot}$ - ray crystallography revealed large square $\mathrm{Ni}_{4}({\bf1})_{4}$ pores of dimensions ca. 11 by $11\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ . $\bf{M}_{4}({\bf1})_{4}^{-}$ squares had previously been shown by Robson et al. to be large enough for interpenetration to occur in the polymer $\mathrm{Zn}({\bf1})_{2}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{2}\mathrm{SiF}_{6}$ .20 However, in the Ni polymer these pores were in fact filled with hydrogen-bonded guest aggregates comprising perchlorate anions, water molecules and additional, uncoordinated 1, and there was no network interpenetration. Hydrogen bonding was found to occur both within these aggregates and between the aggregates and the aquo ligands of the nickel centres, suggesting significant interaction with the framework. Related to this point is a series of 2-dimensional square grid polymers exemplified by [Ni$(\mathbf{1})_{2}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}]$ ·2pyrene.21 Zaworotko and coworkers showed that aromatic molecules such as naphthalene, pyrene, anisole and others, could fill up the cavities in the metal–organic polymer. The aromatic guests were in contact with each other through edge-to-face aromatic interactions, so that they could actually be described as forming a second, non-covalent, network which interpenetrated the coordination polymer (Figure 11). There were also short (face-to-face) contacts between the pyrene guests and the bipyridine ligands of the coordination framework. There was no interpenetration of coordination networks in these compounds.
It is also possible to design frameworks in which interpenetration is forbidden by the inherent dimensions of the framework itself. This relies on the fact that in order to interpenetrate, the size of the vertex or diameter of the polymer strand must be less than the size of the cavity. The use of bulkier vertices could therefore give rise to cavities, as long as there is also control over the ‘natural’ cavity size of the structure (this approach assumes that, even when forbidden from interpenetrating, the metal and ligands will still adopt the expected overall topology). Metal clusters and cages, as opposed to single metal ions, are therefore of interest as building units. Yaghi et al. introduced this idea to coordination networks with the example of an approximately cubic terbium dicarboxylate network, using $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -azodibenzoate (15) as the dicarboxylate linker, where the octahedral $\mathrm{Tb}_{2}$ (carboxylate)6 SBU shown in Figure 12 was adopted.22
This SBU is a structural unit which had previously been characterised in discrete form in terbium complexes of monocarboxylates. Use of the long linker 12 gave rise to very large cubic cavities (ca. $20\mathring\mathrm{A}$ on each side) but the structure was found to be only doubly interpenetrating, and retained a large open volume which was filled by numerous DMSO guests. The explanation for the existence of this free volume was that, despite the very large cavity size, the large bulk of the SBU vertex only allowed a maximum of two networks to mutually interpenetrate, leaving a relatively large volume unoccupied. This assertion was supported by a geometric model of two interpenetrating cubic frameworks with large spherical vertices, which enabled the maximum degree of interpenetration, $n$ , to be related to the diameter of the SBU, $d_{\mathrm{{\Omega}}}$ the length of the linker, $l$ , and the van der Waals radius of the atom joining the SBU and linker, $\delta/2$ (Figure 12).
$$
n\leq\sqrt{3}\frac{d+l}{d+\delta}
$$
This relationship was extrapolated to display the consequences of $d$ and $l$ for the percentage free volume in maximally interpenetrating cubic structures (Figure 12, bottom).
Use of bulky ligands. Use of bulky connecting ligands should also decrease the tendency to interpenetrate. In this approach, window sizes to cavities should be reduced, and the polymer strand which would need to thread through that window is made more bulky. Schröder et al.13d explored this by using 2,7-diazapyrene (16) to prepare the diamondoid network $[\mathrm{Cu}(\mathbf{16})_{2}][\mathrm{PF}_{6}]$ . Only threefold interpenetration occurred, compared to fourfold interpenetration in analogous networks based on 1.13a,b
Although 16 is slightly shorter than 1, which would also decrease the propensity for interpenetration, it was concluded that the major factor was its greater lateral steric bulk. It has also been pointed out that nitriles, being less bulky than pyridines, give greater numbers of interpenetrating strands for similar ligand lengths, probably also for steric reasons.13d,23 Phosphine ligands bearing bulky phenyl groups may have also given rise to the same phenomenon,15c where the network was very bulky indeed. The $X$ -ray crystal structure of $\mathrm{[Ag_{4}({\bf17})_{3}(O T f)_{4}]}$ revealed hexagonal layers with very large 72-membered rings of diameter $16{-}18.4\ \mathrm{nm}$ . Although accurately defining the dimensions of the irregularly-shaped layers is somewhat difficult, it seems that the large thickness and depth of the layers would prevent identical frameworks from orthogonal interpenetration. This was also supported by a simplified geometric model.
Effect of concentration. In perhaps the most dramatic examples of metal-organic frameworks seen so far, Yaghi et al. succeeded in producing a series of materials with square pore windows of diameter up to $19.1\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ , and pore diameters of up to $28.6\ \mathring\mathrm{A}.\mathrm{1}2b$ The calculated densities of these materials were as low as $0.21\mathrm{g}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ – the lowest of any crystalline material. These compounds consisted of rigid dicarboxylate linkers and octahedral $\mathrm{Zn_{4}O(c a r b o x y l a t e)_{6}}$ SBUs connected into cubic frameworks (Figure 13). Whilst doubly interpenetrating frameworks were obtained in some cases, their non-interpenetrating counterparts could be obtained by conducting the synthesis under more dilute conditions.
## 3 Properties
## 3.1 Structural stability and the sorption and exchange of liquids and vapours
Assuming that interpenetration has been controlled and that a material with an appreciable cavity size has resulted, the question of stability arises. In addition to any required counterions, solvent molecules normally become included inside any cavity which is large enough to accept them and they may even template the structure around them. A frequent problem is that networks are not stable to loss of the solvent guests. Here are described some of the frameworks which have shown evidence of stability to guest loss, or at least the ability to reversibly exchange one guest for another without structural collapse. Also included in the discussion are examples where the framework loses crystallinity on guest loss, but regains it on re-exposure to guests.
The high kinetic stability of zeolites is consistent with the high energy barrier to the breaking of strong Si–O and Al–O bonds. Coordination bond strengths are lower and so MOFs are expected to be less thermally stable. This does generally seem to be the case, and typically decomposition occurs at lower temperatures than for zeolites. Framework stability could be maximised by ensuring good hard-hard or soft-soft matching between the ligand and metal to maximise bond strengths, which might help explain why some of the highest decomposition temperatures for coordination polymers have been observed for lanthanide carboxylates.24 However, in studies where TGA has been the sole technique used to determine the decomposition temperature, it is possible that phase changes and loss of long range order occur before weight is lost.
As a general strategy, Robson suggested exploiting the chelate effect to increase the stability of porous coordination polymers.25 Ligand 18 was proposed as a phenanthroline-type chelate which could generate metal-organic polymers via its three divergent chelating sites.
structure with nitromethane molecules and perchlorate anions occupying the pores. Stability studies were encouraging for this chelate approach. A single crystal was left open to the atmosphere, which led to apparent exchange of the nitromethane guests for atmospheric water molecules. Significantly, X-ray diffraction revealed that the product was, however, still crystalline, even though a change from a tetragonal to a cubic unit cell had occurred, as well as an overall decrease in cell volume. Re-exposure to nitromethane caused regeneration of the original cell volume, although the cell remained cubic, and a second exposure to the atmosphere lead again to the smaller cubic cell. This last change was once again associated with exchange of nitromethane for water. The chelate framework was robust enough to persist throughout each of these guest exchanges.
Moore reported an early example of partial guest loss from a stable porous coordination network.5a The polymer $\mathrm{[Ag({\bf19})(O_{3}S C F_{3})]\cdot2C_{6}H_{6}}.$ , contained large cavities with sixteen benzene molecules per unit cell, four of which were disordered. Thermogravimetric analysis revealed mass losses corresponding to four $(110^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ and then twelve $(145~^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ benzene molecules. X-ray powder diffraction showed that the framework remained intact after the first solvent loss, but not after the second. Changes in diffraction intensity indicated that it was the disordered benzenes that had been lost in the first step.
There have also been several metal-organic frameworks reported to undergo complete, or almost complete, desolvation with retention of the framework structure, even if characterisation of the desolvated form by single crystal $\mathrm{\DeltaX}$ -ray crystallography has not been included (for exceptions to the latter see below). Some of the desolvated materials have also been found to resorb small molecules, sometimes with considerable selectivity. Reaction between 1,3,5-benzenetricarboxylic acid $\mathrm{(H}_{3}\mathbf{6)}$ and Co, Ni or $Z\mathrm{n}$ salts gave isostructural hydrated products, $\mathrm{M}_{3}({\bf6})_{2}{\cdot}12\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , with zig-zag polymeric chains and Hbonding between the chains.26 Partial dehydration gave materials which were formulated as monohydrates ${\bf M}_{3}({\bf6})_{2}.{\bf H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ after elemental analysis. X-ray powder diffraction showed these materials to have poor crystallinity. Nevertheless, re-exposure to water regenerated the original structures, perfectly in the case of the Zn compound, but imperfectly for the Co and Ni compounds. The $Z\mathrm{n}$ monohydrate also reacted with ammonia to generate a material which elemental analysis suggested was $\mathrm{\bar{Z}n_{3}(6)_{2}.2H_{2}O\cdot10N H_{3}}$ . There was no reaction with larger potential ligands such as pyridine, which was ascribed to size constraints imposed by a proposed channel structure based on the original dodecahydrate. Under different synthetic conditions, in the presence of pyridine, $\mathrm{\bfH}_{3}\mathrm{\bfBTC}$ reacted with $\mathrm{Co}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}$ to give a different polymer, $\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{H}{\bf6})(\mathrm{NC}_{5}\mathrm{H}_{5})_{2}{\cdot}(2$ / $3)\mathrm{NC}_{5}\mathrm{H}_{5}$ .6 Pyridine was found coordinated at the Co centres forming Co-H6-pyridine layers, but also as an uncoordinated guest, occupying channel spaces in the framework. By heating to $200~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ the uncoordinated pyridines were removed and the resulting material gave an X-ray powder diffraction pattern
A silver coordination polymer $\mathrm{[Ag({\bf18})(C l O_{4})]{\cdot2C H_{3}N O_{2}}}$ was obtained and structurally characterised. It had a porous whose most intense lines were identical in intensity and position to those of the starting material, supporting some retention of the original structure. The material was able to resorb pyridine to regenerate the original material. Amongst other potential guests the material showed selectivity toward aromatic molecules, as deduced from competitive sorption experiments using solvent mixtures such as benzene/nitromethane or benzonitrile/ acetonitrile. This selectivity was ascribed to the ability of aromatic guests to enter into favourable $\pi$ -stacking interactions with the framework.
The silver-triphosphine framework $\mathrm{[Ag_{4}({\bf17})_{3}(O T f)_{4}]^{15c}}$ which has a layered chickenwire structure and unusually wide channels $(1.6\mathrm{-}1.8\ \mathrm{nm})$ ) also showed evidence of retaining its structural integrity after guests had been removed. Thermal decomposition was followed by XRPD, which gave a decomposition temperature between 170 and $200~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . Guest ethanol molecules in the as-synthesised material could be exchanged for other liquids (e.g. diethyl ether) by immersion of the crystals in an excess of that liquid. After immersion in dichloromethane, followed by heating to $170~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 24 hours at $0.01\ \mathrm{mmHg}$ , a sample of the material was dissolved in $\mathrm{CD}_{3}\mathrm{CN}$ and $^{1}\mathrm{H}$ NMR spectroscopy used to ascertain the presence of any guests. There was no ethanol or dichloromethane detected, although traces of water were present. XRPD showed no evidence of amorphous phases, or crystalline phases other than the original.
Examples of stable frameworks which have been structurally characterised by single crystal X-ray diffraction in both solvated and desolvated forms are still quite rare. Rosseinsky et al. described the single crystal structures of $[\mathrm{Ni}_{2}(\mathbf{1})_{3}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{4}]$ in both its ethanol-loaded and empty forms.27a This framework consisted of ‘tongue-and-groove’ bilayers, with $\mathrm{C-H\cdot\cdot\cdotO}$ hydrogen bonds, involving nitrato and bipyridine ligands connecting the layers. The material had channels of ca. 6 by 3 Å which, in the as-synthesised state, were occupied by ethanol molecules hydrogen bonded to nitrato ligands. The ethanol molecules could be completely removed by heating to $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , and then reintroduced by exposure of the desolvated material to ethanol vapour. Single-crystal $\mathbf{\boldsymbol{X}}$ -ray crystallography revealed that only slight structural readjustment occurred in the framework during this process (Figure 14).
The overall framework connectivity was retained on desolvation, with a decrease in the unit cell volume of only $2.4\%$ . A detailed thermodynamic and kinetic analysis of sorption in the desolvated material was also made. $27b$ It revealed complex behaviour with steps in the sorption isotherms. For methanol sorption, steps occurred at high loading which were associated with slowing down in the adsorption kinetics at these points. This was interpreted as being due to the speedy initial occupation of nitrate sites (hydrogen bond acceptors) by methanol molecules at low loadings, followed by a structural adjustment of the framework to allow further methanol sorption to occur after the initial nitrate sites had been saturated.
If the framework synthesis was conducted in methanol solvent, a different structure $\mathrm{[Ni({\bf1})}_{3}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{4}]{\cdot}2\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{OH}$ was obtained, with similar pseudo-square pyramidal coordination at Ni, but connected to form closed-packed metal-bipyridine ladders.27c In sorption studies of this material, considerable framework flexibility was discovered. Although the desolvated compound had a pore window of cross-sectional area $12.3\mathrm{\AA}^{2}$ , toluene, of minimum cross-sectional area $26.6\mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{2}$ could still be sorbed into the structure. This is in marked contrast to sorption in zeolites, where the pore window size is taken as a more rigid indicator of which guests may be admitted.1 Whilst considerable flexing of the framework must have occurred to allow access of the guest through the windows, Monte Carlo calculations showed that once the guest had been included relatively little structural expansion was required to accommodate it within the pores. The ethanol-templated tongue-andgroove structure could be converted to the methanol templated ladder structure by standing in a static methanol vapour phase.
The fact that samples which, after saturation with methanol and subsequent removal from the methanol vapour, did not undergo this structural change suggested that it was surface-adsorbed methanol which was essential to the isomerisation. However, observations of individual crystals using an optical microscope showed that they retained their shape during the process, suggesting that it was not due to dissolution and recrystallisation.
Yaghi et al. reported single crystal structures of $Z_{\mathrm{{n_{4}}}(\mathbf O)(\mathbf{5})_{3}}$ (5 $=1{,}4$ -benzenedicarboxylate) in both solvated and desolvated states, which revealed no change in the unit cell parameters, indicating more inflexible zeolite-like sorption and desorption behaviour.27d
Fujita et al. reported a stable, non-interpenetrated square grid framework which was obtained by reacting the extended bipyridyl-type ligand $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -di(4-pyridyl)biphenyl (20) with $\mathrm{Ni}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}$ in a mixture of methanol and $o$ -xylene solvents, and had the layered structure depicted in Figure $15.^{27e}$
Rectangular channels running orthogonal to the layers were occupied by an ordered array of $o$ -xylene and methanol molecules, which were lost on heating to $70-150~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . The framework retained its integrity up to $300^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . A single crystal structure determination on the empty material revealed that guest loss led to only very slight readjustments of the framework.
However, after immersion in mesitylene, a distinct change occurred and a new structural form was obtained, which was related to the original by a dramatic sliding of the layers over each other. The channels in the latter material were much wider than in the original, and mesitylene was included within them. The structural change was monitored by using X-ray diffraction on a single crystal of the original material sealed in a capillary tube which also contained some mesitylene. This experiment supported the assertion that a genuine crystal-to-crystal transformation had taken place, rather than dissolution and recrystallisation. A second example of a crystal-to-crystal structural change was also reported in a $Z\mathrm{n(m)}$ based framework with the bridging ligand $\pmb{2.^{27f}}$ In this case, a doubly interpenetrated network was obtained which had continuous channels occupied by either nitrobenzene or cyanobenzene molecules, depending on which had been used as solvent in the synthesis. On exposure to the atmosphere, the crystals turned from colourless to yellow, and single crystal $\mathbf{\boldsymbol{X}}$ -ray diffraction revealed that the guests had been lost. Also, significant changes in unit cell parameters had occurred with a reduction in volume of $20\mathrm{-}23\%$ , and associated reduction in parameters such as inter-network metal–metal distances. Re-immersion of this desolvated ‘condensed’ material in nitrobenzene regenerated the original solvent-filled ‘expanded’ framework (Figure 16).
Whilst single crystal X-ray structures have been obtained for both solvated and desolvated forms of certain frameworks, supporting the assertion that permanent porosity can indeed be achieved in metal-organic frameworks, the observation of reversible guest sorption does not in itself indicate that a material has permanent porosity. Work by Wright et al. $28a$ on a series of zinc polymers based on 1,4-benzenedicarboxylate (5) highlighted the fact that metal-organic polymers are potentially highly reactive and adaptable materials. On partial loss of the Hbonding water and DMF content from $[\mathrm{Zn}_{3}({\ }{\pmb5})_{3}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{3}]$ ·4DMF to give $[Z\mathrm{n}(\pmb{5})(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})]$ ·DMF, very dramatic changes in structure occurred, and in fact the two polymeric structures were topologically quite distinct from each other (Figure 17). The structural change must have involved considerable metal– ligand bond breaking and reforming, i.e., loss of the hydrogen bonding molecules caused actual reconstruction of the metal ions and ligands into a new and distinct network topology. Therefore, it should be borne in mind that, even though molecules may be reversibly sorbed in a metal-organic polymer material, the included molecules might not in all cases be most accurately regarded simply as guests within the pores of fixed frameworks, but instead as integral components of dense but structurally adaptable materials. Loss of such guests can even lead to materials with greatly reduced crystallinity which may, however, undergo perfect recrystallisation to the inclusion compound on re-exposure to the guest in vapour form.
Kitagawa reported a $\mathtt{C u}$ -based framework with the 1,2-dipyridylglycol (21) bridging ligand.28b The alcohol groups were not used in coordination to $\mathtt{C u}$ , but instead provided hydrophilic surfaces to channels of $4\times6\mathrm{~\AA~}$ cross-section. The channels were occupied by water molecules which could be removed. In this case the empty material showed an only slightly altered and still sharp XRPD pattern compared to that of the hydrated material, supporting retention of the structure with only a slight change in inter-layer distances. The original diffraction pattern was regenerated on exposure to water or methanol. Interestingly, the methanol sorption isotherm showed hysteresis (Figure 18), consistent with sorption and desorption being associated with structural readjustment. Methane, despite being smaller, was not sorbed by this material.
Related to the issues of stability and guest inclusion is the ability to functionalise a framework after it has been formed.
Lee and co-workers29 reported a remarkable series of structures based on silver ions and bridging trinitriles, which also bore diverse functional groups and spanned a range of sizes. They reported good evidence that one of these channelled structures bearing alcohol functionalities could be converted to the corresponding ester functionalised framework by exposing it to trifluoroacetic anhydride vapour, and that this occurred without rearrangement of the framework.
## 3.2 Other properties
Chirality. Chiral metal-organic frameworks are interesting because they could be applied to enantioselective separations, especially so because of the difficulties in preparing enantiopure traditional zeolite frameworks. The fact that metal-organic frameworks are prepared in a ‘modular’ or ‘building block’ fashion means that chirality can be introduced simply by choosing chiral building blocks, most conveniently as the organic ligands. Rosseinsky et al. reported Ni frameworks based on bridging 6, which also contained diols coordinated at the nickel centres.30a The 1,2-ethanediol-containing network had monodentate diols, and exhibited fourfold interpenetration. Despite this interpenetration, pseudo-tetrahedral pores of diameter $\overset{\cdot}{12}\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ accounted for $28\%$ of the crystal volume, and were interconnected to form continuous channels. The helical nature of the networks meant that the crystals were inherently chiral, bulk samples consisting of 50:50 ratios of enantiomeric crystals. Use of racemic 1,2-propanediol as the alcohol led to a closelyrelated helical structure, but with chelating diols at the nickel centres and only twofold network interpenetration. Individual crystals of this material were again found to grow as single enantiomers, and, interestingly, each crystal contained only one enantiomer of the diol. This suggested that the diol co-ligand had exerted a templating effect on the handedness of the coordination networks. This clearly pointed to the possibility of harnessing the chirality of organic building blocks to produce enantiopure porous metal-organic frameworks. Kim and coworkers used an enantiopure ligand 22 derived from D-tartaric acid to prepare a porous framework on reaction wit $Z\mathrm{n}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}$ .30b The distorted trigonal prismatic SBU in Figure 1 was adopted, and the structure exhibited chiral channels.
Pyridinium groups pointed into the channels, and these could undergo exchange of protons for other cations. The chirality of the material was manifested in its ability to discriminate to some degree between enantiomers of $[\mathrm{Ru}(2,2^{\prime}\mathrm{-bipy})_{3}]^{2+}$ . Immersion of the framework in a methanolic solution of racemic $\mathrm{[Ru}(2,2^{\prime}-$ $\mathrm{bipy}_{3}]^{2+}$ led to a change in the colour of the crystals from colourless to yellow, and a $66\%$ enantiomeric excess of the $\Delta$ form of the complex was found to have been included. The material was further used to demonstrate enantioselective catalysis (see below).
Gas Sorption. Sorption of liquids and vapours has already been discussed above in the section on stability. Sorption of gases has also been used to investigate the effective porosity and apparent surface area of frameworks, and to probe the possibility of future applications in gas storage. For example, Yaghi reported ${\bf N}_{2}$ sorption by $Z_{\mathrm{{n_{4}}}(\mathbf O)(\pmb{5})_{3}}$ at $^{78\mathrm{~K~}}$ and found a reversible type 1 isotherm similar to isotherms observed for most zeolites.27d Strikingly, Kitagawa reported that $\mathrm{Cu}({\bf1})_{2}(\mathrm{SiF}_{6})$ was able to sorb very large quantities of methane.31a In fact, relative to framework weight, more methane was sorbed by this material than by zeolite 5A, which was taken as the optimum conventional zeolite for methane sorption (see Figure 20).
A great impetus for studying this kind of sorption is the need for low-pressure storage media for gases such as methane and hydrogen, due to the potential applications of the latter as ‘clean’ fuels. Subsequently, Yaghi et al. reported still greater weight-for-weight methane sorption of $240\ \mathrm{cm}^{3}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ in a $Z\mathrm{n}$ - carboxylate framework.12b When compared to a conventional gas cylinder, at a pressure of 205 atm, the results suggested that this material could store $70\%$ as much methane in the same volume, but at a lower (safer) pressure of 36 atm.
Very strikingly, sorbed oxygen molecules were recently investigated within a metal-organic framework at $90~\mathsf{K}.^{31b}$ The $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ molcules were located by $\mathrm{\Delta}\mathrm{X}$ -ray crystallography and found to exist as $(\mathbf{O}_{2})_{2}$ van der Waals dimers at an intermolecular distance of 3.28(4) $\mathring\mathrm{A}$ , close to that in solid $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ , despite the study being conducted at well above the pure $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ freezing point of 54.4 K. This illustrates the fact that metal-organic frameworks are providing unusual new microenvironments.
Ion exchange and solubility. When crystals of the silverbipyridyl polymer $[\mathrm{Ag}(\mathbf{1})(\mathrm{NO}_{3})]^{14c}$ were immersed in aqueous solutions of anions such as $\mathrm{PF}_{6}-$ , $\mathbf{MoO}_{4}-$ , $\mathrm{BF_{4}}^{-}$ or $\mathrm{SO}_{4}{}^{2-}$ they were found to undergo anion exchange, i.e. the aqueous anion, which was in excess, was incorporated into the crystals and the crystal’s nitrate anions were released into solution. For $\mathrm{PF}_{6}-$ , the exchange was almost complete after 6 hours, and the crystals had become opaque, with XRPD giving a new diffraction pattern. Re-exposure to a nitrate-containing solution gave back transparent crystals with the original XRPD pattern. This process was later studied32 by microscopy, including TEM and AFM, and with spectroscopic examination of the supernatant. The exchange process was concluded to occur via the solution phase, i.e. there was dissolution of the original polymer and subsequent crystallisation of the new anion-exchanged polymer, rather than ion diffusion and exchange within an inert solid framework (as occurs with aluminosilicate zeolites). Since coordination polymers are normally obtained as crystals from solution, and good quality X-ray crystal structures imply that in the solid the chains are very long indeed, coordination polymers have often been assumed to be effectively insoluble. This is often supported by visual observation of crystal stability on immersion in given solvents. This latest work shows how important it is to examine the supernatant solution. Clearly the stability of coordination polymers to leaching will be a critical point with regard to potential applications in ion-exchange or water/solvent purification.
Luminescence and nonlinear optical (NLO) properties. The combination of size- or shape-selective sorption with luminescence is of interest since it could in principle lead to selective sensor materials. Puddephatt designed luminescence into gold-phosphine coordination polymers by targeting the $\mathrm{Au}(\mathrm{PR}_{3})_{3}+$ structural unit, and preparing the 2-D chickenwire network polymer $[\mathrm{Au}_{2}(\mathrm{Ph}_{2}\mathrm{P}(\mathrm{CH}_{2})_{4}\mathrm{PPh}_{2})_{3}][\mathrm{Au}(\mathrm{CN})_{2}]_{2}$ , which had the desired trigonal $\mathsf{A u P}_{3}$ centres.15b Strong blue-purple solid state luminescence was observed for this polymer, similar to that of the monomeric model complex $[\mathrm{Au}(\mathrm{PPh}_{3})_{3}]^{+}$ . However, the polymer was not porous and no inclusion behaviour was reported. The presence of lanthanide centres in $[\mathrm{Tb}(\pmb{5})_{2}]$ also lead to luminescence.24a This material was able to sorb water and ammonia, each of which gave rise to products with different luminescence decay constants.
Lin et al. exploited the fact that diamondoid networks lack centres of symmetry to prepare two materials which exhibited second order nonlinear optical (NLO) behaviour.33 Odd numbers of interpenetrating diamondoid networks are also noncentrosymmetric, and the latter is a requirement for second order NLO activity. Frameworks exhibiting 3- and 5-fold interpenetration based on $Z\mathrm{n(m)}$ or $\operatorname{Cd}(\operatorname{II})$ and mixed pyridinecarboxylate ligands, were prepared, and found in one case to give a second order NLO response of three times that of potassium dideuterophosphate (KDP).
Catalysis. Catalysis is often cited as a desirable characteristic of metal-organic frameworks, and a justification for general research into these materials. However, there are still relatively few reports of actual catalytic activity, and detailed investigations are particularly lacking. In 1994 Fujita reported that the channelled Cd framework $\mathrm{[Cd}(\mathbf{1})_{2}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}]$ would catalyse the cyanosilylation of aldehydes.34 In particular, treatment of benzaldehyde and cyanotrimethylsilane with a powdered suspension of $\mathrm{[Cd}(\mathbf{1})_{2}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}]$ in dichloromethane at $40^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ over 24 hours gave 2-(trimethylsiloxy)phenylacetonitrile in $77\%$ yield. Importantly, these workers had considered that the reaction might be occurring due to partial dissolution of the framework into catalytically active soluble discrete complexes. Accordingly, they tested the activity of a supernatant dichloromethane phase and found it gave no activity, supporting the contention that catalysis was occurring at the solid. Also, no activity was observed for the separate framework component precursors $\mathrm{Cd}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}$ and 1. Some selectivity was observed in the catalytic behaviour, in that 2-tolualdehyde was a more active substrate than 3-tolualdehyde, and although $\alpha-$ and $\upbeta$ -naphthaldehyde were good substrates, there was almost no conversion of 9-anthraldehyde. This selectivity was taken to be due to the limited cavity size of the material. The selective inclusion of ortho-dihalobenzenes in preference to meta or para isomers was also reported, supporting the material’s selective inclusion characteristics. The cadmium centres are distorted octahedral with $\mathbf{N}_{4}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ coordination sets, and, if coordination to Cd is involved in the catalysis, it implies that there is dissociation of the terminal nitrate anions to give active Lewis acid sites. The enantiopure $Z\mathrm{n}$ -based framework of Kim and coworkers30b (see above) has chiral channels with pyridinium functional groups protruding into the channel space. Accordingly, these researchers tested for catalytic activity in transesterification reactions. They observed catalytic activity and even a modest ca. $8\%$ enantiomeric excess in the product when racemic 1-phenyl-2-propanol was used as the solvent. There was also evidence that the pyridinium groups of the channels could be alkylated with, for example methyl iodide, and this protected nonacidic framework showed little or no activity.
## 4 Conclusions
There is growing evidence to support the optimistic forecasts of the earliest researchers in this field. MOFs are a diverse and rapidly growing class of functional material. The use of organic bridging ligands has allowed the rational design and functionalisation of structures, allowing their properties to be rationally tuned. Demonstrations have been made of chiral frameworks and unusual sorption behaviour distinct from that seen in aluminosilicate zeolites. Despite the newness of the field, dramatic demonstrations, especially of gas sorption capacities, have been reported which suggest that applications may occur. Researchers are now patenting their work.35 In addition, MOFs are providing new confined environments in which to study novel inclusion effects. Lastly, the unusually large cavity sizes that can be accessed with these crystalline compounds, now encroaching into the mesoporous regime of $2\ \mathrm{nm}$ and above, suggests that MOFs may contribute to the bottom-up approach to nanoscale synthesis. For example, they might act as structural templates for the formation of nanowires and other shapes of diverse materials within their pores.
